<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dubays ibn Sadaqa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1135">1135</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dubays ibn Sadaqa was the Mazyadid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">al-Hilla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1108 to 1135, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadaqa-ibn-mansur">Sadaqa ibn Mansur</a>. A powerful Arab chief, he repeatedly fought the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and the Seljuk sultans, allied with Zengi and other rivals of the caliphs, and lost and regained Hilla several times. He was killed in 1135 on the orders of the Seljuk sultan Mas'ud.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dubays, also known as Dubays II, was born in 1070 into the Banu Mazyad, an Arab Shi'ite tribe of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and was named after his great-grandfather Dubays I ibn Ali, emir from 1017 to 1082. His father Sadaqa, emir from 1086, rose to prominence in the troubled reign of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkyaruq">Berkyaruq</a> and was known in early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicles as the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad I Tapar</a> became sultan in 1105 he moved against his powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and Sadaqa was killed in 1108, leaving Dubays the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> and the Middle Euphrates region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dubays preserved his independence at al-Hillah by entering the Seljuk succession wars and changing sides between rival sultans. After Muhammad Tapar died in 1118, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan">Mahmud II</a>'s younger brother Mas'ud rebelled with Dubays' support, but in 1120 Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, with the young officer Zengi among his troops, defeated them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Dubays married Guhar Khatun, daughter of the Artuqid Ilghazi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and in 1121 the two allies suffered a heavy defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a>, barely escaping with their lives.</p><p>He defeated al-Bursuqi in the summer of 1122, but in March 1123 Zengi beat his forces at the Battle of Mubarraqiyya, and an attack he and Mas'ud then made on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was crushed. In 1124 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a>, recently released from captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, routed Timurtash at Marj Dabiq and joined the Christian and Muslim siege of Aleppo that began on 6 October 1124. The city's qadi, Ibn al-Khashshab, denounced him for treason and held out until a relief force under al-Bursuqi arrived on 25 January 1125.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustarshid, trying to restore Abbasid power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, counted him among his chief enemies. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a> captured Dubays and handed him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1130, Zengi offered a captive son of Buri, atabeg of Damascus, in exchange for his release. On 20 June 1132 the two allies were defeated by the caliph's forces at Hisn al-Baramika, north of Baghdad. In his last years Dubays was closely tied to Mas'ud, sultan from 1133: Mas'ud married his daughter Sufra Khatun, and one of Mas'ud's daughters married his son Sadaqa II. He was also known across the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> as a patron of Arabic poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dubays ibn Sadaqa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Dubays</b> allied with Zengi and other enemies of the caliphs.</blockquote><a href="/state/mazyadid-dynasty">Mazyadid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">Hillah</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragheh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.48,44.43&amp;year=1125" aria-label="Hillah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dubays ibn Sadaqa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6030 4722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6030 4722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.48,44.43&amp;year=1125">What was here in 1125? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
